ncient cities. The burial is simultaneously the agent of partial damage (cracks, collapse of upper walls) and the sole agent of long-term survival of the remaining fabric. ### Have I changed my position? I haven't changed my underlying explanation, but I should have been clearer from the start that "preservation" doesn't mean "like new." I was focusing on "why aren't these things crushed to powder?" and you were focused on "you said they were intact, but now you admit they're cracked." You're right that "intact" was sloppy language on my part. I meant "still standing in recognizable form," not "unscathed." So to your closing question: **"aren't you just calling the destroyed parts we haven't lost sight of yet 'preserved'?"** Yes, that's essentially accurate—with one crucial modification. I'm calling the parts we still see *partially destroyed but structurally stable enough to survive*. The burial's net effect is that it destroys the fragile upper parts but saves the robust lower parts from the utter erasure that befalls an exposed barn. The debris giveth and the debris taketh away. You're right that it's a destructive process, but it's a destructive process whose final product, paradoxically, is a standing wall rather than a pile of dust.
